  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are derived from a single DRT service in southern Hamburg, which may limit their direct applicability to cities with different transit structures or spatial layouts.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the influence of built environment characteristics on demand-responsive transport (DRT) demand using 2024 trip data from southern Hamburg, Germany. Spatial regression models were applied to small-scale grid cells across five distinct time-of-week periods to capture temporal variations. The analysis revealed that rail stations, hospitals, and population density consistently drive demand, while schools, workplaces, and retail locations show highly time-dependent effects.",
      "why": "It demonstrates exactly when and where DRT acts as a complement to conventional transit rather than a competitor, showing that poor fixed-route transit access only boosts DRT demand during off-peak hours when regular service is weak."
    }
  },
